# --- Rust / Tauri ---
target/
**/*.rs.bk
*.pdb

# Written by tauri-build on every build, and named after the host platform —
# `macOS-schema.json` here, `windows-schema.json` on a Windows machine. Editor
# schemas for the capability files, derived entirely from the installed plugins.
# Committing them would put a rebuild in every diff and one machine's platform
# in everyone's tree. The icons under src-tauri/icons ARE committed, because
# `generate_context!` will not compile without them.
/src-tauri/gen/schemas/

# --- Node / the copied React interface ---
node_modules/
dist/
.vite/

# --- Python, for the experiments only ---
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.venv/

# --- third-party prebuilt binaries ---
# The Pdfium shared library is 7.7 MB and reproducible: scripts/fetch-pdfium.sh
# fetches a pinned release and verifies its checksum before installing. The pin
# lives in that script and in crates/mnema-extract, which is where it belongs —
# committing the binary would put a copy of the decision in a place nobody reads.
# Anchored: an unanchored `vendor/` matches a directory of that name at any depth,
# and the React interface being ported from the server repository is exactly the
# kind of tree that could end up hiding source under one.
/vendor/

# --- staged sidecars ---
# scripts/stage-sidecar.sh rebuilds this on every `cargo tauri build`, through
# beforeBuildCommand. Committing it would let a stale worker ship inside a green
# build, and nothing downstream could tell the difference — verify-bundle.sh
# checks freshness for exactly that reason.
/src-tauri/binaries/

# --- model artefacts and run output ---
# A single full experiment run writes ~2.3 GB of ONNX external-data tensors.
# All of it is reproducible from the scripts plus the model snapshot.
models/
_out/

# --- secrets ---
# Model-provider keys belong in the OS credential store (§4.5), never in a file.
# This line is a backstop, not the mechanism.
.env*
!.env*.example

# --- OS ---
.DS_Store

# local-only operator guidance for Claude Code (not committed), same as the server repository
CLAUDE.md

# The design document and the workflow output it comes from live in a separate private
# repository, cloned here as docs/private/. Nothing under it is part of this repository:
# §N and D<n> references in the comments point into it, and are citations rather than
# missing pieces. A matching `!docs/private/` in .ignore keeps ripgrep able to read it.
/docs/private/

# Two symlinks into it, not directories — which is why neither pattern has a
# trailing slash: a symlink is a file to git, and `docs/superpowers/` would not
# have matched one. They exist so the design tooling keeps writing to the paths
# it knows while the files land in the private repository, under version
# control. Before the split those two paths were ignored and in no repository
# at all: `.superpowers/sdd/` held 84 files of task briefs and reviews under a
# `.gitignore` containing `*`, and one `git clean` would have taken them.
docs/superpowers
/.superpowers
